;; PCI configuration space, write half.
;;
;; The companion to `pci-config-read`. Same address word to port 0xCF8 (3320),
;; then the caller's value out to port 0xCFC (3324) instead of a read. See
;; `pci-config-read.kotoba` for the layout of the address word and why it is
;; built here rather than in C; only what differs is stated below.
;;
;; It is a separate object, not a fifth argument to the read, because the two
;; are not the same operation: one observes and one mutates. Keeping them apart
;; means the mutating one can be denied, audited or fuel-bounded on its own,
;; and it keeps each export at the arity the kernel-object contract fixes.

;; DOMAIN AND FAILURE
;;
;; Same four field-width checks as the read -- bus <= 255, dev <= 31, fn <= 7,
;; offset <= 255 -- and an out-of-domain call performs NO port I/O and returns
;; 0 instead of 1.
;;
;; The check matters MORE here than on the read side. An over-wide field
;; carries into its neighbour in the address word -- dev 32 sets bit 16, the
;; low bit of the bus field; fn 8 sets bit 11, the low bit of the device field
;; -- so the failure mode of an unchecked write is not a wrong answer: it is a
;; write to the configuration space of a device that was never named,
;; rewriting a BAR, a command register or an MSI-X control word on hardware
;; the caller did not intend to touch. That is unrecoverable in a way a bad
;; read is not, so the write half stops before the first `out`. (The offset
;; cannot carry -- `bit-and offset 252` truncates it -- but it can silently
;; wrap onto the wrong register of the right function, which for a write is
;; still a mutation nobody asked for.)
;;
;; The result is 1 on success and 0 on refusal, so a caller can tell the two
;; apart. Today's C wrapper is `void` and drops it; the value is returned
;; anyway because the refusal is a fact about the call, and a caller that grows
;; a reason to check should not have to change this object to do it.
;;
;; `value` is NOT range-checked. It is the payload, not an address field: it
;; occupies the whole data word and cannot carry into anything, and `out` takes
;; the low 32 bits of `eax` regardless. There is nothing to decide about it
;; here -- what a given register will accept is the device's rule, not this
;; object's.

(defn aiueos-pci-config-write [bus dev fn-index offset value]
  (if (or (< bus 0) (> bus 255)
          (< dev 0) (> dev 31)
          (< fn-index 0) (> fn-index 7)
          (< offset 0) (> offset 255))
    0
    ;; Both bindings are sequencing: each is evaluated exactly once, in order,
    ;; so the address word reaches 0xCF8 before the data word reaches 0xCFC.
    ;; Neither written value is used.
    (let [addressed (kernel-out-u32 3320
                                    (bit-or 2147483648
                                            (bit-or (* bus 65536)
                                                    (bit-or (* dev 2048)
                                                            (bit-or (* fn-index 256)
                                                                    (bit-and offset 252))))))
          written (kernel-out-u32 3324 value)]
      1)))

(defn main [] 0)
